title: 'fix(xcm): charge precise barrier-check weight on barrier rejection'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    On barrier rejection the executor previously charged the full message weight, over-estimating
    the work done. This adds an optional `WeightBounds::barrier_check_weight()` hook (default
    `None`): a weigher that provides it has that precise weight charged on rejection, otherwise the
    previous behaviour is kept, so existing `XcmConfig`s are unaffected.

    The weight is measured by two `pallet_xcm_benchmarks::generic` benchmarks,
    `barrier_check_ref_time` and `barrier_check_proof_size` (driven by new
    `worst_case_barrier_check_ref_time` / `worst_case_barrier_check_proof_size` config methods).
    A barrier's worst case can split into disjoint paths each worst in a different dimension, so
    each is benchmarked separately and combined with a component-wise `Weight::max`; a barrier
    worst in both dimensions can implement just one method. Westend and its system parachains
    (Asset Hub, Bridge Hub, Collectives, Coretime, People) wire this into their `Weigher`.

    Since this weight is a worst-case constant it can exceed a cheap message's execution weight, so
    `PreparedMessage::weight_of()` now reports the maximum of the two. This keeps it an upper bound
    on what the executor consumes, so a caller reserving `weight_of()` (e.g. the `MessageQueue`)
    never over-consumes on rejection; weighers without `barrier_check_weight()` are unaffected.

    Closes #7965.
